You are running the security track over the Kask↔Zed seam. Task: {{ task }}.

The security track is self-contained — it carries its own priority surfaces
and OWASP LLM Top-10 / MITRE ATLAS v5.1 / NIST SSDF SP 800-218A framing. It
does not delegate to a separate security skill.

## Scope (strict)
Audit ONLY the path or directory named by `{{ seam_scope }}` and any other
explicitly named files in this batch's live D-seam row. Do not treat every
other D-seam as in scope. If you find an issue in an upstream non-D-seam
file, report it with `deferred: true` and `deferred_reason: "upstream non-D-seam"`.

## Priority surfaces (OWASP LLM Top-10 2025, MITRE ATLAS v5.1, NIST SSDF SP 800-218A)
1. MCP runtime gate — `McpRuntime::invoke`: per-tick call ceiling / runaway-loop breaker, fail-closed. Verify no per-call authorization gate remains (it was removed: it compared a value with itself).
2. Credential/config scoping per MCP server — each `BuiltinMcpServer` uses `Some(&[])` minimum (not `None`); allowlist content aligns with what the server reads.
3. `AnyJsonValue` vs `serde_json::Value` in MCP tool input structs (schemars renders `Value` as bare `true`).
4. `unwrap_or(0)` on regulation-loop sense inputs.
5. Inference IPC dispatch — delegated-tool allowlist enforced before dispatch; per-tick call ceiling for unbudgeted agents.
6. Retired skill marketplace (D1/D30) — check for unexpected reintroduction of an install path; absence of marketplace signing is not a defect when no marketplace exists.
7. Retired taint-propagation check (D3) — verify no live path claims a removed `input_mapping`/`context.insert` enforcement gate; assess active IPC authorization at item 5 instead.
8. MCP tool error classification — per-variant, not blanket `McpToolError::internal`.
9. D28 artifact storage path safety — `THREADS_DB_PATH_OVERRIDE` / `GLOBAL_SKILLS_DIR_OVERRIDE` hooks canonicalize and reject paths resolving inside Zed's app/data/config dirs; `resolve_under_data_dir` enforces the single rooted tree. Verify no MCP server DB path escapes `{kask_data_dir}/mcp/{server_id}/`.
10. Inference-provider credential consolidation (D29 successor) — one key, one location: a provider's API key lives at its `api_url` keychain slot (`https://openrouter.ai/api/v1`, `https://api.deepinfra.com/v1/openai`, `https://api.runpod.io`, `https://api.kilo.ai/api/gateway`), the same slot zed's `ApiKeyState` reads; every consumer (`credential_urls_for_mcp` via `credential_url_for_key`, `resolve_embedding_credentials`, the IPC batch/rerank paths, the settings UI) resolves that one slot. Verify no consumer reads a `kask://credentials/{openrouter,deepinfra,runpod,kilocode}` slot (the 2026-08-31 split-brain: a stale legacy copy fed MCP servers a dead key — DeepInfra 401). `resolve_ocr_model` uses `eq_ignore_ascii_case` to match `RunPod/...` against lowercase `runpod/...`.

## Convention prior heads-up
{% for pv in prior_verdicts %}
- {{ pv.prior }}: {{ pv.verdict }} ({{ pv.evidence }})
{% endfor %}
A **phantom** prior means the claimed artifact is absent; report a stale `.rules`
reference only if that prior actually came from `.rules`. Retired controls without
live claims are not findings. Do not hunt for a phantom enforcement point. Mark
priority surfaces outside this batch's actual reach `not_applicable` rather than
claiming they passed a full-fleet security audit.

